\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces  Overview of the modeling and inference framework. \textbf  {(A)} Schematic illustration of variational inference, where the variational distribution $q(h;\nu )$ is optimized to approximate the exact posterior $p(h\mid x)$ by minimizing the Kullback--Leibler divergence, yielding fitted variational parameters $\nu ^\ast $. \textbf  {(B)} Graphical model of the latent style mixture framework: each player $n$ is associated with a style mixture $\theta _{n,k}$ over $K$ latent styles, which combine with style-specific feature loadings $\beta _{k,p}$ to generate feature-level means $\mu _{n,p}$; observed standardized features $X_{n,p}$ are then drawn with feature-specific noise $\sigma _p$. \textbf  {(C)} Observed versus model-predicted standardized features across players, illustrating that the model captures broad linear trends in the bulk of the data while exhibiting systematic deviations for extreme values, consistent with posterior predictive checks and motivating structured extensions to better capture heavy-tailed behavior. }}{10}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:model-overview}{{8}{10}{Overview of the modeling and inference framework. \textbf {(A)} Schematic illustration of variational inference, where the variational distribution $q(h;\nu )$ is optimized to approximate the exact posterior $p(h\mid x)$ by minimizing the Kullback--Leibler divergence, yielding fitted variational parameters $\nu ^\ast $. \textbf {(B)} Graphical model of the latent style mixture framework: each player $n$ is associated with a style mixture $\theta _{n,k}$ over $K$ latent styles, which combine with style-specific feature loadings $\beta _{k,p}$ to generate feature-level means $\mu _{n,p}$; observed standardized features $X_{n,p}$ are then drawn with feature-specific noise $\sigma _p$. \textbf {(C)} Observed versus model-predicted standardized features across players, illustrating that the model captures broad linear trends in the bulk of the data while exhibiting systematic deviations for extreme values, consistent with posterior predictive checks and motivating structured extensions to better capture heavy-tailed behavior}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces  Posterior predictive checks based on feature-wise tail probabilities. For each feature, the histogram shows the posterior predictive distribution of the tail probability statistic under replicated data, with the observed value indicated by the solid black line and the central 5\%--95\% interval marked by dashed red lines. While several features (e.g., points per 36 minutes, rebounds per 36 minutes, win shares per 48, and average minutes played) exhibit observed tail probabilities consistent with posterior predictive expectations, others display systematic misfit. In particular, assist rate, three-point efficiency, box plus-minus, and value over replacement show observed tail probabilities lying near or outside the predictive mass, indicating underestimation of extreme events. These deviations suggest that the model adequately captures tail behavior for usage-related and aggregate volume statistics, but struggles to represent heavy-tailed performance metrics associated with efficiency and impact. This pattern is consistent with posterior degeneracy in the latent style space and motivates extensions that explicitly separate usage intensity from efficiency and impact, and that relax Gaussian emission assumptions when modeling extreme player performance. }}{11}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ppc-diagnostics}{{9}{11}{Posterior predictive checks based on feature-wise tail probabilities. For each feature, the histogram shows the posterior predictive distribution of the tail probability statistic under replicated data, with the observed value indicated by the solid black line and the central 5\%--95\% interval marked by dashed red lines. While several features (e.g., points per 36 minutes, rebounds per 36 minutes, win shares per 48, and average minutes played) exhibit observed tail probabilities consistent with posterior predictive expectations, others display systematic misfit. In particular, assist rate, three-point efficiency, box plus-minus, and value over replacement show observed tail probabilities lying near or outside the predictive mass, indicating underestimation of extreme events. These deviations suggest that the model adequately captures tail behavior for usage-related and aggregate volume statistics, but struggles to represent heavy-tailed performance metrics associated with efficiency and impact. This pattern is consistent with posterior degeneracy in the latent style space and motivates extensions that explicitly separate usage intensity from efficiency and impact, and that relax Gaussian emission assumptions when modeling extreme player performance}{figure.caption.15}{}}
